  code={https://github.com/Lamn17/DVAL},
  bibtex_show={true},
  preview={DVAL.png},
  ABSTRACT="Traffic sign detection (TSD) is important for intelligent transportation
systems and autonomous driving, but training reliable detectors requires
costly manual annotation of bounding boxes and class labels. To reduce this
effort, we propose Detector and Vision-Language Active Learning (DVAL), a
two-stage active learning (AL) framework for TSD. AL reduces annotation
cost by iteratively selecting the most informative unlabeled samples under
a limited labeling budget. In the first stage, a You Only Look Once (YOLO)
detector is trained on the current labeled set. Based on these predictions,
we introduce Detection Difficulty Uncertainty Sampling (DDUS), which
estimates detection uncertainty based on class-level classification and
localization difficulty to select candidate images from the unlabeled pool.
In the second stage, YOLO-predicted boxes are used to crop traffic sign
regions, which are processed by Multi-modal Prompt Learning (MaPLe) as a
vision-language prompt learning module. The entropy from MaPLe and the
prediction mismatch between YOLO and MaPLe are combined into an image score
to select the final query images for annotation. Experiments are conducted
on two TSD datasets, namely the German Traffic Sign Detection Benchmark
(GTSDB) and the Vietnamese Traffic Sign Detection Database 100 (VTSDB100),
using YOLO11-Nano as the detector. With 50\% of the training images
labeled, DVAL achieves 0.9762 mAP(at)50 and 0.8419 mAP(at)50:95 on GTSDB,
compared with 0.9813 and 0.8535 from fully supervised training. On
VTSDB100, DVAL achieves 0.9616 mAP(at)50 and 0.7593 mAP(at)50:95, compared
with 0.9702 and 0.8158 from fully supervised training. DVAL also achieves
the highest mAP(at)50 among the compared AL methods on both datasets,
showing competitive detection performance with fewer labeled images."
}
